WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.130
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:09.188
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.488 --> 00:00:16.169
Unit seventeen: Global History. Today you will widen the historical frame with 'beyond' + noun phrase.

4
00:00:16.469 --> 00:00:18.912
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.867 --> 00:00:23.030
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.883 --> 00:00:25.117
Globalization.

7
00:00:26.117 --> 00:00:30.636
Globalization means the increasing connection of the world's economies and cultures.

8
00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:34.376
Globalization is not only a modern phenomenon.

9
00:00:34.983 --> 00:00:38.170
Your turn. Say: Globalization.

10
00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:42.603
Globalization.

11
00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.456
Interconnection.

12
00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:49.029
Interconnection means a link between different places or systems.

13
00:00:49.617 --> 00:00:52.011
Ancient ports reveal deep interconnection.

14
00:00:52.617 --> 00:00:55.223
Your turn. Say: Interconnection.

15
00:00:58.067 --> 00:00:59.172
Interconnection.

16
00:00:59.917 --> 00:01:01.045
Trade network.

17
00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:06.035
Trade network means a system of routes and partners for exchanging goods.

18
00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:08.882
Funan was part of a vast trade network.

19
00:01:09.483 --> 00:01:12.670
Your turn. Say: Trade network.

20
00:01:15.533 --> 00:01:17.068
Trade network.

21
00:01:17.817 --> 00:01:19.212
Cultural exchange.

22
00:01:20.217 --> 00:01:24.863
Cultural exchange means the sharing of ideas, art and customs between cultures.

23
00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:28.611
Cultural exchange brought Sanskrit to Southeast Asia.

24
00:01:29.217 --> 00:01:32.136
Your turn. Say: Cultural exchange.

25
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.489
Cultural exchange.

26
00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:38.460
World system.

27
00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:43.957
World system means an economic system linking regions into core and periphery.

28
00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:47.978
World-system theory explains unequal trade.

29
00:01:48.583 --> 00:01:51.131
Your turn. Say: World system.

30
00:01:53.967 --> 00:01:55.328
World system.

31
00:01:56.083 --> 00:01:57.061
Empire.

32
00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.687
Empire means a group of territories ruled by a single power.

33
00:02:02.283 --> 00:02:05.084
The Mongol empire connected Asia and Europe.

34
00:02:05.683 --> 00:02:08.092
Your turn. Say: Empire.

35
00:02:10.933 --> 00:02:11.842
Empire.

36
00:02:12.583 --> 00:02:13.782
Transnational.

37
00:02:14.783 --> 00:02:18.629
Transnational means extending across national borders.

38
00:02:19.217 --> 00:02:21.808
Buddhism is a transnational religion.

39
00:02:22.417 --> 00:02:25.127
Your turn. Say: Transnational.

40
00:02:27.967 --> 00:02:29.258
Transnational.

41
00:02:30.017 --> 00:02:31.250
Interdependence.

42
00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:35.736
Interdependence means mutual reliance between countries.

43
00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:38.907
Economic interdependence can discourage war.

44
00:02:39.517 --> 00:02:42.587
Your turn. Say: Interdependence.

45
00:02:45.433 --> 00:02:46.690
Interdependence.

46
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:51.464
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:52.317 --> 00:02:57.625
Visal, why should Cambodian students study global history, not just Khmer history?

48
00:02:58.717 --> 00:03:09.333
Because our own story makes sense only beyond a single nation's borders. Funan, for example, traded with India, China and even the Roman world.

49
00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:11.776
How do we know that?

50
00:03:12.883 --> 00:03:22.862
Roman coins were found at Oc Eo. That evidence points beyond local politics, towards a trade network stretching across the Indian Ocean.

51
00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:26.031
And what spread along those routes?

52
00:03:27.117 --> 00:03:38.140
Far more than goods: Hinduism, Buddhism and Sanskrit came through cultural exchange. Global history looks beyond borders to those connections.

53
00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.649
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:41.483 --> 00:03:44.330
Grammar focus: Beyond plus noun phrase.

55
00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:45.782
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:46.483 --> 00:03:49.818
Global history looks beyond a single nation's story.

57
00:03:50.717 --> 00:03:53.378
The evidence points beyond local politics.

58
00:03:54.283 --> 00:03:57.862
Its influence extended far beyond the borders of the empire.

59
00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:08.047
'Beyond' + noun phrase means 'further than' a limit: beyond borders, beyond doubt, beyond our control. Intensify it with 'far' or 'well': 'far beyond the region'.

60
00:04:08.783 --> 00:04:12.957
Be careful! Don't say: Global history looks beyond of one nation.

61
00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:18.362
Say: Global history looks beyond one nation.

62
00:04:18.662 --> 00:04:20.163
'Beyond' needs no 'of'.

63
00:04:20.967 --> 00:04:23.491
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:25.719
Connected Pasts.

65
00:04:26.019 --> 00:04:31.327
Global history challenges the assumption that the nation-state is the natural unit for studying the past.

66
00:04:32.333 --> 00:04:37.990
Instead, it looks beyond borders to the trade networks, migrations and ideas that linked distant societies.

67
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:45.667
Long before modern globalization, the Indian Ocean connected East Africa, Arabia, India and Southeast Asia.

68
00:04:46.667 --> 00:04:52.730
The spread of Buddhism, for example, can only be understood by tracing exchanges across many regions.

69
00:04:53.733 --> 00:05:00.342
By highlighting interdependence, global history shows that no society has ever developed in complete isolation.

70
00:05:01.433 --> 00:05:05.574
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:06.417 --> 00:05:09.229
The temple's influence reached far blank Cambodia.

72
00:05:09.529 --> 00:05:13.101
A, beyond. B, beyond of. C, above of.

73
00:05:16.667 --> 00:05:18.434
The answer is A: beyond.

74
00:05:19.583 --> 00:05:21.351
Choose the correct sentence.

75
00:05:21.651 --> 00:05:28.903
A, The problem is beyond of our control.. B, The problem is beyond our control.. C, The problem beyond is our control..

76
00:05:32.467 --> 00:05:36.068
The answer is B: The problem is beyond our control..

77
00:05:37.217 --> 00:05:40.586
The sharing of ideas and customs between cultures is blank.

78
00:05:40.886 --> 00:05:45.306
A, world system. B, cultural exchange. C, empire.

79
00:05:48.867 --> 00:05:51.760
The answer is B: cultural exchange.

80
00:05:52.917 --> 00:05:55.497
Mutual reliance between countries is blank.

81
00:05:55.797 --> 00:06:00.913
A, interdependence. B, transnational. C, globalization.

82
00:06:04.467 --> 00:06:07.279
The answer is A: interdependence.

83
00:06:08.417 --> 00:06:12.251
According to the reading, global history shows that no society…

84
00:06:12.551 --> 00:06:17.957
A, has used trade. B, developed in complete isolation. C, had borders.

85
00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:24.753
The answer is B: developed in complete isolation.

86
00:06:25.967 --> 00:06:28.793
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:33.391
What is one example of cultural exchange that shaped world history?

88
00:06:50.183 --> 00:06:52.044
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:52.344 --> 00:07:32.063
One example of cultural exchange that shaped world history is the spread of Buddhism from India across Asia. Carried by monks and merchants, it influenced art, philosophy and politics far beyond its place of origin. In Cambodia, it eventually became the dominant religion and shaped our values, festivals and architecture. Some historians stress that such exchanges were often unequal or connected with power. Even so, I think this example shows how ideas can travel and transform entire civilizations.

90
00:07:32.363 --> 00:07:34.061
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:39.541
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and beyond plus noun phrase.

92
00:07:39.841 --> 00:07:45.233
Today's value is global awareness. Remember that every story is connected to the wider world.

93
00:07:45.533 --> 00:07:48.244
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:48.544 --> 00:07:51.707
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

